<div class="vl-how">
  <h2 class="vl-section-title">How decryption works</h2>
  <ol class="vl-steps">
    <li class="vl-step">
      <span class="vl-step-num">1</span>
      <div class="vl-step-body">
        <strong>Paste the ciphertext</strong>
        <p>Your colleague or sender gives you a base64-encoded ciphertext string created by Secret Lock. Paste it into the input field.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="vl-step">
      <span class="vl-step-num">2</span>
      <div class="vl-step-body">
        <strong>Enter the shared passphrase</strong>
        <p>Type the passphrase that was used to encrypt the secret. This should have been communicated to you through a separate, trusted channel (not the same place you received the ciphertext).</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="vl-step">
      <span class="vl-step-num">3</span>
      <div class="vl-step-body">
        <strong>Client-side key derivation and decryption</strong>
        <p>Your passphrase is run through PBKDF2 (100,000 iterations, SHA-256) to derive the same 256-bit AES key used during encryption. The IV is extracted from the ciphertext header. AES-GCM authentication is verified — if the passphrase is wrong or the data is tampered, decryption fails with an explicit error.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="vl-step">
      <span class="vl-step-num">4</span>
      <div class="vl-step-body">
        <strong>Plaintext appears locally</strong>
        <p>The decrypted secret appears on your screen. Nothing was sent to any server — all cryptographic operations ran in your browser via the Web Crypto API.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>
</div>

